<p>Indian soybean meal typically costs more than Brazilian, Argentine or US meal — Indian yields are lower and strong domestic crush demand for oil keeps export prices elevated. German feed buyers look at it anyway for one reason: India doesn't permit commercial cultivation of genetically modified soybean, so Indian meal is non-GMO by origin — not through a certification scheme, but structurally, because the crop grown in India isn't GM in the first place. For German and broader European feed programmes serving non-GMO-labelled meat, dairy and poultry supply chains, that's a characteristic price competition from South America can't replicate. This guide is for those buyers: how to specify the meal, what the German paperwork involves, how the price is built, and where first orders go wrong.</p>

<!-- slot:facts -->

<p>On volume, the sources disagree. The Soybean Processors Association of India named Germany among India's top-five soybean-meal destinations for the OMY2024-25 marketing year, alongside France, Nepal, Bangladesh and Kenya, but gave no per-country figures to verify. The primary WITS/UN Comtrade calendar-2023 data in the table above records only a small German figure. Trade to Germany likely moves in large, infrequent shipments, and EU statistics sometimes record cargo against the Rotterdam or Hamburg gateway rather than the final buyer's country. Volume here is real but not evenly distributed, and the non-GMO reason to buy from India holds regardless of where Germany ranks by tonnage in any given year.</p>

<!-- insight:spec-to-application -->
<h2>Which protein grade suits German feed rations, 46% or 48%?</h2>
<p>46% and 48% minimum crude protein are the two standard export grades, solvent extracted. German buyers, in our experience, specify tightly and expect a proper certificate of analysis — moisture, crude fibre, crude fat, sand/silica content and urease activity, which indicates whether heat treatment during processing was correctly calibrated. Have your specification precise before requesting a quote; vague specs slow the process down on this market more than most.</p>
<p>The choice follows the ration. 48% meal, traded as Hi-Pro, packs more protein into each tonne, so it suits compound feed for broilers and piglets, where the formulator needs high protein density without more fibre. 46% meal carries a little more hull and fibre and suits ruminant and layer rations, where that fibre is not a problem and the lower price matters more. The two-point gap is worth working out per tonne of protein, not per tonne of meal: 1,000 kg of 48% meal delivers 480 kg of crude protein against 460 kg from 46% meal, about 4.3% more. If the Hi-Pro premium on your quote is above roughly 4%, the 46% grade is the cheaper protein, unless your formulation needs the density.</p>
<p>Scenario: a dairy feed mill in Lower Saxony buying for cattle rations specifies 46% and puts its money into tighter urease and moisture limits instead. A broiler integrator next door specifies 48% Hi-Pro, because every point of protein in the ration it does not get from meal has to come from a dearer ingredient.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

<!-- insight:container-economics -->
<h2>How much soybean meal fits in a 20ft container to Hamburg?</h2>
<p>About 24-26 MT bagged in a 20ft, and 26-28 MT in a 40ft. Both are weight-limited, not volume-limited. Standard packing is 25kg, 40kg or 50kg PP bags, or 1 MT jumbo bags (FIBC) for buyers with bulk intake handling. Transit to Hamburg runs 18-24 days; Bremerhaven is the secondary gateway some buyers prefer for onward inland logistics.</p>
<p>Because the payload, not the space, sets the limit, a 40ft adds only about 2 MT: 27 MT against 25 MT at the midpoints, or 8% more meal. A 40ft is the cheaper box per tonne only if its freight costs less than 1.08 times the 20ft rate to Hamburg. Divide each quoted rate by its tonnage before choosing.</p>
<p>Packing changes the intake cost, not the tonnage. A 25 MT container is 1,000 bags of 25kg, 500 bags of 50kg or 25 jumbo bags. A mill that unloads by forklift and empties FIBCs into a pit moves 25 lifts instead of 1,000 bags, which is why jumbo bags suit mills with bulk intake. For annual volumes of several thousand tonnes, bulk vessel loading spreads freight over a whole ship rather than 25 MT boxes, at the cost of a larger single commitment. Our default MOQ is one 20ft container, so a first trial can run on 24-26 MT.</p>
<!-- /insight -->

