<h2>India vs. South American origin — a fair comparison</h2>
<p>Brazil and Argentina dominate global soybean meal trade by volume and typically price below Indian meal. The comparison below is about origin characteristics, not a claim that one is simply "better" — the right choice depends on whether non-GMO origin is a requirement for your supply chain or a preference.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead><tr><th scope="col">Factor</th><th scope="col">India</th><th scope="col">Brazil / Argentina</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>GMO status</td><td>Non-GMO by origin — India does not permit commercial GM soybean cultivation</td><td>Majority GM soybean cultivation; non-GMO lots exist but are segregated, certified and priced at a premium</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical export price level</td><td>Generally priced above South American origins</td><td>Generally the lower-cost global benchmark</td></tr>
<tr><td>Protein grades typically offered</td><td>46% / 48% min, solvent extracted</td><td>Comparable protein ranges available, both solvent-extracted and expeller meal</td></tr>
<tr><td>Transit to Hamburg</td><td>18-24 days</td><td>Longer transit from South American East Coast ports in most routings</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical buyer profile for this origin</td><td>Non-GMO-labelled feed programmes, smaller/specification-driven orders</td><td>Bulk commodity feed buyers, price-driven high-volume orders</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Where Brazil and Argentina genuinely win: price, scale and bulk logistics. They are the benchmark that Indian meal lost 11% of its export volume against in 2024-25, according to SOPA, and segregated non-GMO lots from South America exist for buyers who need them. Where India wins: GMO status is settled by what the country grows, not by segregation along a supply chain, and transit to Hamburg is 18-24 days. Scenario: a German integrator running a VLOG-labelled poultry line and a conventional line buys Indian meal for the first and South American meal for the second, rather than paying India's premium on both.</p>
